<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      The theme every ModalSheet's window is built from, and it exists for
      exactly one reason: a backdrop blur is gated on the window being
      TRANSLUCENT, and "translucent" here is a THEME attribute, not a format
      and not a window type.

      This is the correction of a finding that cost the project several
      sessions and was wrong. `Window.setBackgroundBlurRadius` was said to be
      impossible for a Dialog because "a Dialog is ty=BASE_APPLICATION and a
      floating window is TYPE_APPLICATION_OVERLAY, which needs
      SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW". That is not where the gate is. AOSP's own
      documentation ("Window blurs", source.android.com/docs/core/display/
      window-blurs) implements the effect on an ACTIVITY with a
      `windowIsTranslucent` theme, and lists dialogs and bottom sheets as the
      feature's intended hosts.

      What actually happens, read from the framework (DecorView.java,
      updateBackgroundBlurRadius):

          mBackgroundBlurRadius = mCrossWindowBlurEnabled && mWindow.isTranslucent()
                  ? mOriginalBackgroundBlurRadius : 0;

      and PhoneWindow.isTranslucent() is nothing but the `android:windowIsTranslucent`
      theme attribute, resolved once in generateLayout:

          mIsTranslucent = a.getBoolean(R.styleable.Window_windowIsTranslucent, false);

      ModalSheet passed `android.R.style.Theme_DeviceDefault_NoActionBar`, an
      opaque ACTIVITY theme, so `isTranslucent()` answered false and the
      radius we set was forced to 0 before it ever reached SurfaceFlinger.
      The call landed, the log said `blur=true`, and the compositor was never
      asked for a region, which reads exactly like a capability failure and
      is not one. `setFormat(PixelFormat.TRANSLUCENT)`, which the code had,
      sets the surface format; it does not touch `isTranslucent()`.

      The transparent `windowBackground` is the second half. DecorView layers
      the blur UNDER the window background
      (`LayerDrawable([blur, mOriginalBackgroundDrawable])`), so an opaque
      window background paints over the blur it just asked for. The card's own
      scrim is a child view drawn on top of both, which is the translucent
      layer AOSP's guidelines say the blur must be read through.

      No `windowIsFloating`: that attribute only decides whether a blur is
      clipped to the window's bounds or drawn fullscreen, and this window IS
      fullscreen (MATCH_PARENT x MATCH_PARENT, and the scrim fills it), so
      fullscreen is the answer we want. Setting it would change the window's
      layout path for no gain.
    -->
    <style name="ModalWindow" parent="@android:style/Theme.DeviceDefault.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowIsTranslucent">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@android:color/transparent</item>
    </style>
</resources>
